A matrix $\mathbf{A}$ is not invertible if and only if $\det (\mathbf{A}) = 0$.

We can calculate the determinant of the given matrix as:
\begin{align*}
\det (\mathbf{A}) & = (1 + x)(8) - (7)(3 - x) \\
& = 8 + 8x - 21 + 7x \\
& = 15x - 13
\end{align*}
We want to find the value of $x$ such that $\det (\mathbf{A}) = 0$, so we set the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x$:
\begin{align*}
15x - 13 & = 0 \\
15x & = 13 \\
x & = \frac{13}{15}
\end{align*}

The answer is:
\[ x = \boxed{\frac{13}{15}} \]

