Find the derivative of $f(x) = x - x^3$:
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 1 - 3x^2
\end{align*}

The critical points are where $f'(x) = 0$:
\begin{align*}
1 - 3x^2 = 0
\end{align*}
Thus, the critical points are $x = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$ and $x = -\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$.

Now, check the endpoints of the interval and compare with the critical points:
\begin{align*}
f \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \right) &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} - \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\right)^3 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} - \frac{1}{3\sqrt{3}} = \frac{2}{3\sqrt{3}} = \frac{2 \sqrt{3}}{9} \\
