Since $x + y + z = 0,$ we have $z = -x - y.$  Then, we can find that
\begin{align*}
x^5 + y^5 + z^5 &= x^5 + y^5 - (x + y)^5\\
&= x^5 + y^5 - (x^5 + 5x^4 y + 10x^3 y^2 + 10x^2 y^3 + 5x y^4 + y^5)\\
&= -5x^4 y - 10x^3 y^2 - 10x^2 y^3 - 5x y^4
\end{align*}

Also, we know $xy + xz + yz = xy - x(x+y) - y(x+y) = -x^2 -xy -y^2$. Therefore
\begin{align*}
xyz (xy + xz + yz) &= xy(-x-y)(-x^2-xy-y^2)\\
&=xy(x+y)(x^2+xy+y^2)
\end{align*}

Putting it all together gives:
\begin{align*}
\frac{x^5 + y^5 + z^5}{xyz (xy + xz + yz)} &= \frac{-5x^4 y - 10x^3 y^2 - 10x^2 y^3 - 5x y^4}{xy(x+y)(x^2+xy+y^2)}\\
&= \frac{-5xy(x^3+2x^2y+2xy^2+y^3)}{xy(x+y)(x^2+xy+y^2)}
\end{align*}

Since $x+y+z=0$, we know $x+y=-z$. Since $z\neq0$ we know $x+y\neq0$. Then $xy \neq 0$ and $x^2+xy+y^2 = (x+y)^2-xy\neq0$ so we can simplify this fraction by dividing by $xy$ and $x^2+xy+y^2$ to find
\begin{align*}
\frac{x^5 + y^5 + z^5}{xyz (xy + xz + yz)} &= \frac{-5(x+y)(x^2+xy+y^2)}{(x+y)(x^2+xy+y^2)}\\
&= -5
\end{align*}

There is only one possible value for this expression.
\[ \frac{x^5 + y^5 + z^5}{xyz (xy + xz + yz)} = \boxed{-5} \]

