Let's add the three congruences together:
\begin{align*}
6a + 6b + 6c &\equiv 8 \pmod{7}\\
6(a+b+c) &\equiv 1 \pmod{7}\\
-(a+b+c) &\equiv 1 \pmod{7}\\
a+b+c &\equiv 6 \pmod{7}
\end{align*}

Now, we add this to each of the congruences:
\begin{align*}
2a+3b+4c &\equiv 6 \pmod{7},\\
3a+4b+2c &\equiv 3 \pmod{7},\\
4a+2b+3c &\equiv 3 \pmod{7}.
\end{align*}

We see that the LHS of each congruence is a permutation of the others, so the RHS's must be the same. Since $3 \equiv -4 \pmod{7}$, we must have $a+b+c \equiv 6 \pmod{7}$. Therefore, $\{a,b,c\} = \{1,2,3\} \pmod{7}$, and the remainder when $abc$ is divided by $7$ is $\boxed{6}$.

